  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe cold front has pushed south of the area early this evening.\nA shortwave is allowing some light rain to quickly spread east\nacross the area north of the front. Somewhat dry low-levels mean\nthere's a bit more bark than bite to the radar, with any rain\namounts under 0.05. The shortwave exits east overnight, taking\nthe rain with it and allowing skies to clear with light winds.\nNot an ideal setup for widespread dense fog early Friday morning,\nbut there will likely be at least some in typical low-lying/ \nless urban areas that are most prone to radiational cooling fog.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nAfter a brief break from both humidity and rain chances on \nFriday, shower and thunderstorm chances will return to the area \nSaturday as a warm front lifts through the region. The greatest \nprobability of showers and thunderstorms will be across southern\nand eastern areas during the day. The potential for gusty winds\nin thunderstorms is still there, but confidence is low due to \nlikelihood of daytime cloud cover and uncertainty with the \nplacement/timing of the best instability and forcing. Dew points\nwill once again reach the upper 60s and lower 70s on Saturday \nso it will be quite humid. Isolated showers can't be ruled out \nas a cold front attempts to cross the region on Sunday with dry\nweather favored on Monday as high pressure briefly builds in \nfrom the west. Another period of unsettled weather is likely as \na series of disturbances ripple across the region Tuesday \nthrough at least Wednesday, though there is still low confidence\nin the exact timing/placement of showers and thunderstorms and \nany resulting impacts/hazards.",
